<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paithan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pratishthana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paithan is a town on the Godavari river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. As ancient Pratishthana it was one of the main cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, named in early Buddhist texts and known to Greek and Roman traders as Paithana. It served as a capital of the Satavahana dynasty and remains a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving, famous for its Paithani saris.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Paithan lies 56 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a>, on the left bank of the Godavari River, in Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar district, at an elevation of about 458 metres. Its old name, Pratiṣṭhāna, means standing firmly. It is one of the few inland towns named in the 1st-century Greek Periplus of the Erythraean Sea. The mounds round the town have yielded prehistoric and protohistoric remains and, from the historical period, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, terracottas, bangles and coins of the Satavahana age, along with punch-marked coins older than the Satavahanas and foreign coins that show its links with the western world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Pratishthana was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simuka">Simuka</a>, the first Satavahana king, and the town kept its importance until the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadavas</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> say it was founded by King Sudyumna, son of Manu, who as Ila established the Somavamsa there; his son Pururavas ruled after him. Under the name Pishtapura it was taken by the Chalukya ruler Pulakesin II, who had the event recorded in a poem. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-religion">Vedic religion</a> all flourished there, and the medieval saints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnyaneshwar">Dnyaneshwar</a> and Eknath, who wrote in simple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, made it a centre of the bhakti movement.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> of the 17th century valued Paithan as a place of pilgrimage and trade. In 1679 Chhatrapati Shivaji halted there on his way to Jalna and issued a charter making Kawale, a leading priest of the town, a royal priest, an arrangement his successors long honoured. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwas</a> kept close ties: Peshwa Balaji Bajirao married into the Wakhare family, moneylenders of Paithan, in 1761, and Peshwa Madhavrao's letters show his regard for the town's textiles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Paithan is a town with a municipal council. Pilgrims gather each year at the shrine of Saint Eknath for the Paithan yatra, and its Digambar Jain temple of Munisuvrata, the 20th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">tirthankara</a>, is an old place of pilgrimage. The Jayakwadi Dam nearby holds a bird sanctuary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paithan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Passes through the Western Ghats, such as the Naneghat, linked inland towns like <b>Pratishthana</b>, the modern Paithan, and Tagara, the modern Ter, with ports at Kalyan and Sopara, while the eastern territories opened on to the Bay of Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its neighbour Mulaka lay around <b>Pratishthana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/assaka">Assaka</a></li></ul></section>
